==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm}} {{term|luminem|lang=enm}}, {{term|limnen||to illuminate (a manuscript)|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|frm}} {{term|enluminer|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|illumino|illūminō|I illuminate|lang=la}} (English {{term|illuminate|lang=en}}), {{term|in|lang=la}} + {{term|lumino|lūminō|light up|lang=la}}, from {{term|lumen|lūmen|light|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|lewk-}} (English {{term|leam||glow|lang=en}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|lĭm}}, {{IPA|/lɪm/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/lIm/}}
* {{rhymes|ɪm}}
* {{homophones|lim|limb}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive}} To [[draw]] or [[paint]]; [[delineate]].
# {{transitive}} To [[describe]].
# {{transitive|obsolete}} To [[illuminate]], as a manuscript.

====Usage notes====
* Particularly used in arts journalism; not understood by significant portion of American public.<ref>[http://www.baltimoresun.com/news/maryland/bs-md-limn-headline-20100907,0,3496485.story The Sun goes out on a limn with unusual headline: Unusual word in front-page headline leaves some readers scratching their heads], Jill Rosen, ''The Baltimore Sun,'' September 7, 2010</ref>

